ial of this commonly used analgesic.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Multiple high-risk populations are contraindicated for 对乙酰氨基酚 use\",\n            \"explanation\": \"对乙酰氨基酚 is explicitly contraindicated for several vulnerable groups, including嗜酒者,长期嗜酒者,长期饮酒者,慢性肝肾功能不全患者,营养不良者, and young children (both两岁以下儿童 and四岁以下儿童). For example, evidence states that 'therapeutic doses of acetaminophen in alcohol users can cause severe liver damage' [Data: Relationships (6244)]. Similarly, 'long-term alcohol users or those with pre-existing chronic hepatic or renal insufficiency should use such drugs cautiously' [Data: Relationships (6252, 6247)]. Nutritional deficiency exacerbates risk due to reduced glutathione stores needed to detoxify NAPQI [Data: Relationships (6250)]. Pediatric use is also restricted, with warnings against use in children under two or four years based on fatal overdose cases involving combination cold medicines [Data: Relationships (6242, 6245)]. These contraindications highlight the drug’s narrow safety margin in susceptible subpopulations.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"CYP2E1 induction significantly increases 对乙酰氨基酚 toxicity\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The interaction between 对乙酰氨基酚 and CYP2E1 inducers—such as chronic alcohol consumption—markedly elevates hepatotoxicity risk. Chronic alcohol intake induces CYP2E1 enzyme activity, which accelerates the conversion of acetaminophen into the toxic metabolite NAPQI. In parallel, alcohol-associated malnutrition often leads to glutathione depletion, impairing the body's ability to neutralize NAPQI. As one source explains: 'Long-term alcohol use induces CYP2E1, increasing the production of hepatotoxic hydroxylated metabolites from acetaminophen; combined with glutathione deficiency in alcohol users, this predisposes to liver damage' [Data: Relationships (25991)]. This pharmacokinetic interaction transforms standard therapeutic doses into potentially lethal exposures in habitual drinkers, making CYP2E1 induction a critical risk amplifier.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"N-乙酰半胱氨酸 is indicated as an antidote for 对乙酰氨基酚-induced liver damage\",\n            \"explanation\": \"N-乙酰半胱氨酸 (NAC) serves as a specific treatment for liver injury resulting from 对乙酰氨基酚 overdose, particularly in cases progressing to肝坏死. Clinical guidelines reference a 'standard nomogram of plasma acetaminophen concentration versus time after ingestion' to determine the need for immediate antidotal therapy. If measured levels fall on or above the treatment line, NAC administration is recommended without delay [Data: Relationships (18888)]. NAC works by replenishing glutathione stores, thereby facilitating detoxification of NAPQI and preventing further hepatocellular damage. Its inclusion in this network as an indicated therapy for liver necrosis directly ties it to the core toxicity pathway of acetaminophen [Data: Entities (2958, 16900); Relationships (18888)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Combination cold medicines containing 对乙酰氨基酚 pose cumulative toxicity risks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Drugs such as感冒灵 and维C银翘 contain 对乙酰氨基酚 and carry warnings about concurrent use with other analgesics. Both are documented to interact with解热镇痛药 (antipyretic-analgesic drugs), increasing the risk of nephrotoxicity and, by implication, hepatotoxicity if total acetaminophen intake exceeds safe thresholds. For instance,感冒灵 'when used with other antipyretic-analgesics, poses increased risk of kidney toxicity' [Data: Relationships (38290)]. Similarly,维C银翘 carries an identical warning [Data: Relationships (47830)]. Given that consumers may unknowingly combine multiple over-the-counter products containing acetaminophen, these interactions create a hidden risk of unintentional overdose, contributing to acute liver injury or failure—a phenomenon documented in U.S. pediatric fatalities [Data: Relationships (11336)].\"\n       